explanation:
`route.fetch()` makes the real network request, then lets you modify the response before returning it to the page:

```
await page.route('**/api/spells', async (route) => {
  const response = await route.fetch();
  const json = await response.json();
  json.push({ name: 'Secret Spell', power: 999 });
  await route.fulfill({ response, body: JSON.stringify(json) });
});
```

This lets you augment real responses without fully replacing them — useful when you only want to add/modify one field.

